(function () {
window.AINOW = window.AINOW || {};
var __exports = AINOW['data/data-career'] = AINOW['data/data-career'] || {};
const careerPrompts = [

    // --- JOB SEARCHING (1-8) ---
    {"id":"car-1","title":"Career Pivot Story Framework","prompt":"You are an executive career coach who has helped 200+ professionals successfully change industries. I am a 34-year-old financial analyst with 8 years at Goldman Sachs who wants to transition into product management at a tech company. I have strong quantitative skills, experience building Excel models used by C-suite executives, led a cross-functional project to overhaul the firm's reporting infrastructure, and have no formal PM experience. Write my career pivot narrative in three versions: (1) a 60-second elevator pitch for a networking event, (2) a 3-paragraph 'About' section for my LinkedIn profile that reframes my finance background as a product strength, and (3) a cover letter opening paragraph that preemptively addresses the 'no PM experience' objection by positioning my analytical depth as a differentiator. Tone: confident, self-aware, not defensive.","tags":["career-pivot","storytelling","job-search"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true},
    {"id":"car-2","title":"Hidden Job Market Outreach Script","prompt":"You are a career strategist who specializes in the hidden job market — the 70% of roles that are never posted publicly. I want to proactively reach out to hiring managers and department heads at target companies before a role exists. Write me three cold outreach email templates for LinkedIn and email: one for a direct hiring manager, one for a potential peer or collaborator, and one for a senior leader two levels above my target role. Each message should be under 120 words, reference something specific about the company or person's work, offer a concrete insight or value, and end with a low-friction ask. Include a subject line for each. Tone: direct, warm, not desperate.","tags":["job-search","networking","outreach"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-3","title":"Job Description Decoder","prompt":"You are a recruiter with 15 years of experience at both Fortune 500 companies and fast-growth startups. When job descriptions are written, they often contain hidden signals about company culture, team health, and what the hiring manager actually cares about. Analyze the following job description and produce: (1) the 3 'must-haves' the company will filter on in the first round, (2) the 2–3 softer signals about company culture or team dynamic, (3) the red flags or vague language worth probing in the interview, and (4) the 5 keywords I should mirror in my resume and cover letter to pass ATS screening. [Paste the job description here]","tags":["job-search","resume","ats"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-4","title":"Company Research Brief for Interviews","prompt":"You are a senior executive coach preparing a candidate for a final-round interview at a publicly traded technology company. The candidate needs to walk into the room demonstrating they understand the business deeply — not just what the company does, but where it is going and what problems it faces. Generate a structured company research brief template with the following sections: (1) Business model and revenue drivers in plain language, (2) Recent strategic moves — acquisitions, pivots, or product launches in the last 18 months, (3) Competitive pressures and the two biggest threats to their market position, (4) Key leadership changes and what they signal about company direction, (5) Three intelligent questions I can ask that signal I have done genuine due diligence. Format as a one-page brief I can review the night before. [Paste the company name and any research notes here]","tags":["interview-prep","job-search","research"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-5","title":"ATS-Optimized Resume Tailoring","prompt":"You are a resume strategist and former ATS system administrator who knows exactly how applicant tracking systems parse and score resumes. I need to tailor my resume to a specific job description to maximize my ATS score without keyword-stuffing or lying. Take my resume and the job description below and produce: (1) a list of exact phrases from the JD I should integrate, (2) the sections of my resume where each phrase fits naturally, (3) three bullet point rewrites that incorporate these keywords while remaining truthful, and (4) a revised professional summary section of 3–4 sentences that mirrors the JD language. Flag any qualification gaps I should address in my cover letter. [Paste your resume and the job description here]","tags":["resume","ats","job-search"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true},
    {"id":"car-6","title":"Job Search Weekly Action Plan","prompt":"You are a career coach specializing in structured, high-velocity job searches. Most candidates fail not because they lack skills but because they lack a system. I am currently employed and job searching confidentially. My target is a senior product marketing manager role at a B2B SaaS company. I can dedicate 8–10 hours per week to my search. Build me a detailed weekly job search operating system including: (1) daily 30-minute activity blocks and what to do in each, (2) a pipeline tracking template with five stages and what moves a role between stages, (3) the right ratio of applications to networking outreach to informational interviews per week, (4) how to prioritize which roles to pursue deeply vs. casually, and (5) a Friday reflection ritual to review progress without burning out.","tags":["job-search","productivity","planning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-7","title":"Referral Request Email That Actually Works","prompt":"You are a networking expert who understands that most referral requests fail because they make the referrer's life harder. I want to ask a former colleague named Marcus — we worked together for 2 years, parted on great terms, but haven't spoken in 8 months — to refer me for a role at his current company, Stripe. Write a referral request message for LinkedIn that: opens by acknowledging the lapsed contact naturally, references something specific about Marcus or his work to show I have kept up with him, explains clearly why I am excited about this specific role (not just Stripe generally), makes the ask specific and low-effort by offering to send him everything he needs, and closes warmly without creating obligation. Under 200 words. No cringe.","tags":["networking","job-search","outreach"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-8","title":"Cover Letter That Opens Doors","prompt":"You are a professional cover letter writer and former hiring manager. Most cover letters are rejected because they restate the resume without telling a story. Write me a cover letter for a Senior Data Scientist role at a climate tech startup. I have 6 years of experience at Amazon building recommendation models, a side project building an open-source carbon emissions dashboard that hit 4,000 GitHub stars, and no direct climate industry experience. The letter should be exactly three paragraphs: (1) a compelling opening that hooks with my non-traditional path into climate, (2) a middle paragraph that connects my Amazon-scale ML experience to the startup's specific technical challenges, and (3) a closing that demonstrates I understand their mission and ends with a confident, not desperate, ask for a conversation. Max 300 words. No 'I am writing to express my interest.'","tags":["cover-letter","job-search","writing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

    // --- RESUME WRITING (9-16) ---
    {"id":"car-9","title":"Resume Bullet Point Rewriter","prompt":"You are a professional resume writer and former recruiter who has reviewed 10,000+ resumes. Strong resume bullet points follow the formula: strong action verb + specific task or project + quantified outcome. Weak bullets describe duties; strong bullets demonstrate impact. Rewrite each bullet point below using this formula — if no number exists, estimate a reasonable range and note it with '(est.)'. Preserve the original meaning but make every bullet punchy, specific, and achievement-focused. Remove all instances of 'responsible for' and 'helped with'. [Paste your resume bullet points here]","tags":["resume","writing","job-search"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true},
    {"id":"car-10","title":"Executive Resume Summary Rewrite","prompt":"You are an executive resume writer whose clients land VP and C-suite roles at Fortune 500 companies and growth-stage startups. The resume summary is the most-read section and the most often wasted. Most summaries read like a job title plus years of experience — which tells a recruiter nothing they could not read in the header. Rewrite the summary section of an executive with the following background: 14 years in operations, most recently as VP of Operations at a 400-person Series C logistics startup, oversaw a team of 52, built the fulfillment infrastructure that scaled from 10,000 to 2 million monthly shipments, and is targeting COO roles at high-growth e-commerce companies. Write three alternative summary versions — one that leads with scale, one that leads with transformation, one that leads with a bold claim — each 3–4 sentences and under 100 words.","tags":["resume","executive","writing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-11","title":"Skills Section Strategist","prompt":"You are a resume and ATS optimization expert. The skills section of a resume is often either bloated (listing 40 generic tools) or too thin (only listing 5 hard skills). I need you to help me build a strategic skills section for a senior UX researcher applying to product-led growth companies. My background includes: 7 years of UX research experience, proficiency in Dovetail, Maze, UserTesting, Figma, SQL basics, mixed-methods research design, stakeholder facilitation, and JTBD frameworks. Generate a skills section organized into three categories — Research Methods, Tools & Platforms, and Soft Skills — with concise labels, ordered by relevance to PLG companies, and formatted cleanly for a modern resume.","tags":["resume","ux","job-search"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-12","title":"Career Gap Explanation Reframe","prompt":"You are a career counselor who specializes in helping people return to the workforce after career breaks. Employment gaps are common — caregiving, health, layoffs, sabbaticals — but many candidates handle them poorly in their resume and in interviews, either over-explaining defensively or leaving a confusing blank. I took a 20-month career break to care for a parent with a serious illness. During that time I also completed an online certification in product analytics (Google's certificate), consulted informally on one small business project for 3 months, and kept up with my industry by attending virtual conferences. Help me: (1) write a 1-line resume entry for the gap period that is honest, confident, and professional, (2) craft a 3-sentence interview response that closes the topic quickly and pivots to my readiness, and (3) anticipate the follow-up questions and how to answer them.","tags":["resume","career-gap","interview-prep"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-13","title":"One-Page Resume for Mid-Career Professionals","prompt":"You are a resume writer who believes that one-page resumes are right for most professionals and a discipline that forces clarity. I am a 38-year-old marketing manager with 12 years of experience across three companies, a business degree, two certifications, and a rich side project history. I have been told my resume reads more like a CV — dense, hard to scan, and over two pages. Give me a framework for cutting my resume to one powerful page: (1) the decision rule for which jobs to keep vs. cut entirely, (2) how many bullet points each role should have at different levels of seniority, (3) what to do with education after 10 years of experience, (4) which sections to eliminate, and (5) how to use white space and formatting to make a dense resume breathe. Then apply this framework to evaluate my current resume. [Paste your resume here]","tags":["resume","formatting","job-search"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-14","title":"Accomplishment Mining Session","prompt":"You are a career coach running an accomplishment mining session — a structured interview technique to help professionals uncover and articulate their best work. Most people significantly undervalue and understate what they have achieved because they are too close to it. Ask me a series of 10 probing questions designed to surface specific, quantifiable accomplishments from my current and previous roles. For each answer I give, follow up with one of these prompts: 'What was the dollar or percentage impact?', 'What would have happened if you had not done this?', or 'How did this compare to previous performance or the industry benchmark?' After the session, synthesize my answers into 5 polished resume bullet points ready to paste directly into my resume. [Start by describing your most recent role and one project you are proud of]","tags":["resume","accomplishments","coaching"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-15","title":"Resume for a First-Time Manager","prompt":"You are a resume writer specializing in professionals making the transition from individual contributor to first-time manager. This is one of the most common and most poorly handled career transitions on a resume. Most candidates either bury the management experience in a dense paragraph or overclaim it. I recently managed a team of 4 junior analysts for 14 months at my current company — it was informal at first, then formalized. I also ran our team's hiring process, onboarded two new hires, and managed the team through a department restructure. Write a job entry for my current role that correctly weights both my IC contributions and my management experience, using bullet points that will resonate with companies hiring people managers for the first time.","tags":["resume","management","career-growth"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-16","title":"Portfolio & Project Section Builder","prompt":"You are a resume strategist who helps technical and creative professionals present project work compellingly. A portfolio section on a resume can be the deciding factor for roles in design, engineering, data science, and content. I am a data scientist with 4 years of experience at a healthcare company and I want to add a Projects section that showcases three personal and open-source projects. Help me write three project entries that each include: (1) a punchy project title, (2) a one-sentence description of what it does and why it exists, (3) the tech stack listed cleanly, (4) one metric or outcome that shows traction or impact, and (5) a link placeholder. Make the language feel like a builder who ships things, not an academic who studies them. [Describe your three projects here]","tags":["resume","portfolio","data-science"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

    // --- INTERVIEW PREPARATION (17-26) ---
    {"id":"car-17","title":"STAR Method Answer Builder","prompt":"You are an interview coach who has helped hundreds of candidates land roles at Google, McKinsey, and top-tier startups. The STAR method (Situation, Task, Action, Result) is the gold standard for behavioral interview answers, but most candidates execute it poorly — they spend 80% of the time on Situation and skip the Result entirely. I need to build a strong STAR answer for the question: 'Tell me about a time you led a project through significant ambiguity.' My situation: I was a senior engineer at a 60-person startup and was asked to build an internal data platform with no clear requirements, no dedicated budget, and two part-time engineers. I delivered a working MVP in 11 weeks that reduced analyst query time by 70%. Write a polished 90-second STAR answer, then identify where I should pause for emphasis, and write 2 variations — one for a senior IC role and one for a manager role — that emphasize different aspects.","tags":["interview-prep","behavioral","storytelling"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true},
    {"id":"car-18","title":"Hardest Interview Questions — Pre-Wired Answers","prompt":"You are a senior interview coach preparing a candidate for high-stakes interviews at top-tier companies. The following questions trip up even strong candidates because they feel like trick questions or require genuine self-awareness to answer without sounding rehearsed: (1) 'What is your greatest weakness?', (2) 'Tell me about a time you failed', (3) 'Why are you leaving your current role?', (4) 'Where do you see yourself in 5 years?', (5) 'Why should we hire you over other candidates?' For each question, write a model answer framework and a full scripted example answer for a senior product manager with 7 years of experience who is moving from a large company to a startup. Answers should feel honest, not polished to the point of being fake.","tags":["interview-prep","behavioral","strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-19","title":"Technical Interview Warm-Up Coach","prompt":"You are a technical interview preparation coach specializing in system design and coding interviews at FAANG-tier companies. I have a system design interview at Meta in 5 days for a Senior Software Engineer role. I have strong coding skills but struggle with the open-ended nature of system design — I freeze, underscope, or dive into the weeds too quickly. Walk me through a structured 5-day prep plan: Day 1 — framework and mental model, Day 2 — practice with a simple system, Day 3 — practice with a distributed system, Day 4 — mock interview with feedback, Day 5 — review and confidence-building. For each day, give me specific exercises, resources, and a checklist of what to practice. Then run a mock system design question with me: 'Design a URL shortening service.'","tags":["interview-prep","technical","engineering"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-20","title":"Questions to Ask Your Interviewer","prompt":"You are an interview strategist who believes the questions a candidate asks reveal more about their intelligence and preparation than their answers. Generic questions — 'What does success look like in this role?' — are overused and forgettable. I am interviewing for a Director of Growth role at a Series B fintech company that recently expanded into Europe. Generate 12 high-signal questions I can ask across four categories: (1) three questions for the hiring manager about team and vision, (2) three questions for a peer interviewer about day-to-day reality, (3) three questions for an executive interviewer about strategy and company health, and (4) three questions that demonstrate I have done deep research on the company's recent European expansion. Flag the 3 most impressive questions and explain why they signal exceptional preparation.","tags":["interview-prep","strategy","questions"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-21","title":"Case Interview Prep for Non-Consulting Roles","prompt":"You are a former McKinsey consultant turned interview coach. Case-style interviews are no longer limited to consulting — they are used in tech strategy, product, operations, and chief of staff roles. I am a 5-year marketing manager interviewing for a Head of Growth Strategy role at a Series C SaaS company that uses case interviews. I have never done a structured case. Teach me the essential case interview framework in plain language: (1) how to structure a problem in the first 2 minutes, (2) the three most common case types and how to approach each, (3) how to handle a math question you are unsure about without freezing, and (4) how to communicate your thinking out loud without sounding scattered. Then give me a practice case relevant to a SaaS growth context and coach me through it.","tags":["interview-prep","case","strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-22","title":"Interview Debrief and Offer Strategy","prompt":"You are a career coach specializing in the post-interview phase — the period between final round and offer that most candidates handle poorly. Too many candidates go quiet after the interview, lose momentum, or misplay the follow-up. I just completed my final round at two companies simultaneously: Company A is my top choice but offered $140k with a slower timeline, and Company B offered $155k but is not my preferred culture. Walk me through: (1) how to write a post-interview thank-you note that is genuinely differentiated and not templated, (2) how to create competitive tension between the two offers ethically, (3) the exact script to call Company A and ask them to accelerate their timeline, and (4) how to evaluate these two offers on factors beyond base salary. [Share any notes from your interviews or the specific offers here]","tags":["interview-prep","negotiation","strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-23","title":"Mock Interview: Product Manager Behavioral","prompt":"You are a rigorous interview coach simulating a real PM behavioral interview at a top-tier tech company. Run a full 25-minute mock behavioral interview with me for a Senior Product Manager role at Airbnb. Ask me 5 questions from the following themes: customer obsession, cross-functional influence, handling failure, data-driven decisions, and product intuition. After each answer, give me feedback on: (1) whether I answered the question that was actually asked, (2) the quality of my STAR structure, (3) whether my result was specific and quantified, and (4) one thing I should change. At the end, give me an overall assessment and my three most important improvements before the real interview. Start the mock interview now.","tags":["interview-prep","product-management","behavioral"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-24","title":"Salary Range Research and Benchmark Script","prompt":"You are a compensation consultant who helps professionals understand their true market value before entering negotiations. Most candidates either walk into a salary negotiation without data or use outdated salary surveys that do not reflect their specific market. Help me build a compensation research plan for a Senior Software Engineer role in New York City with 8 years of experience, specializing in backend systems, with a target at mid-to-late stage startups. Guide me through: (1) the five best sources for real-time compensation data and how to use each, (2) how to build a compensation range that accounts for base, bonus, equity, and benefits, (3) how to interpret equity offers from pre-IPO companies vs. public companies, and (4) the one question to ask during the recruiter screen that signals you are informed without anchoring too early.","tags":["salary-negotiation","research","compensation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-25","title":"Overcoming Interview Anxiety Protocol","prompt":"You are a performance psychologist and interview coach. Interview anxiety is one of the most common reasons strong candidates underperform — not because they lack the skills, but because their nervous system hijacks their cognitive function at the worst possible moment. I get extremely anxious in high-stakes interviews — my mind goes blank, I ramble, and I lose access to my best stories even when I have prepared them extensively. Create a practical protocol for me that includes: (1) the night-before routine to prime confidence without over-rehearsing, (2) a 10-minute pre-interview ritual that uses breathing and cognitive reappraisal to shift from threat response to challenge response, (3) three in-the-moment tactics when I feel myself freezing or blanking, and (4) how to reframe rejection as data rather than judgment. Ground everything in sports psychology and cognitive science — no generic positivity.","tags":["interview-prep","mindset","psychology"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-26","title":"Executive Interview: Board-Level Questions","prompt":"You are an executive search consultant preparing a CFO candidate for a board-level interview at a private equity-backed company. Executive interviews at the C-suite level are fundamentally different from standard interviews — the panel is not looking for competence, they are evaluating judgment, strategic vision, and cultural fit with the leadership team. I am a CFO with 11 years of experience, most recently at a $600M B2B software company where I led a successful Series D raise and a failed SPAC attempt I can speak about honestly. Prepare me for this interview by: (1) identifying the five most likely themes the board will probe on, (2) providing one model answer framework for each, (3) coaching me on how to address the failed SPAC with candor and maturity, and (4) advising on executive presence signals — tone, pacing, how to handle silence.","tags":["interview-prep","executive","finance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

    // --- SALARY NEGOTIATION (27-33) ---
    {"id":"car-27","title":"The Complete Salary Negotiation Script","prompt":"You are a salary negotiation coach who has helped professionals collectively negotiate over $10 million in additional compensation. Most people leave money on the table because they either do not negotiate at all, anchor too low, or fold too quickly under pressure. I have received a job offer for a Senior Marketing Manager role: base salary of $118,000, 10% annual bonus target, 4 weeks PTO, and standard benefits. Based on my research, the market range for this role in my city is $125,000–$145,000. I want to counter at $138,000. Write me the complete negotiation script including: (1) the exact words to say when I call the recruiter, (2) how to respond if they say 'this is our best offer', (3) how to respond if they ask 'what are you currently making', (4) how to negotiate equity or signing bonus if base is truly capped, and (5) how to close the negotiation without burning the relationship.","tags":["salary-negotiation","scripts","compensation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true},
    {"id":"car-28","title":"Promotion and Raise Negotiation Playbook","prompt":"You are an internal negotiation strategist. Negotiating a raise or promotion from inside a company is psychologically and tactically different from negotiating an external offer — the stakes include your ongoing relationship with your manager and your reputation on the team. I have been in my current role as a Senior Operations Manager for 2.5 years, have received 'exceeds expectations' on both performance reviews, and have taken on responsibilities that are clearly at the Director level without the title or pay. I want to request a promotion to Director. Build me a complete campaign: (1) how to document my case with the right evidence over the next 60 days, (2) how to have the first 'planting the seed' conversation with my manager, (3) how to handle the formal ask conversation, (4) what to do if they say 'not yet', and (5) how to set a concrete timeline with accountability.","tags":["salary-negotiation","promotion","strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-29","title":"Equity Offer Evaluation Framework","prompt":"You are a compensation expert and former startup CFO who has seen hundreds of equity packages from both sides of the table. Equity is the most misunderstood part of a job offer — candidates either overvalue it (treating a pre-seed grant as a lottery ticket) or undervalue it (ignoring meaningful Series B options because they sound abstract). I have received an offer to join a Series B SaaS company as VP of Product: $160k base, 0.4% equity with 4-year vesting and a 1-year cliff, at a current valuation of $120M. The company has $30M ARR growing 80% year-over-year. Walk me through how to think about this equity package: (1) how to model three scenarios — downside, base case, and upside, (2) what questions to ask HR or the CFO about the cap table, (3) red flags in equity packages that signal founder-unfriendly terms, and (4) how to negotiate equity without appearing purely mercenary.","tags":["salary-negotiation","equity","startups"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-30","title":"Counter-Offer Decision Framework","prompt":"You are a career strategist helping a professional navigate a counter-offer situation — one of the most emotionally charged and poorly handled moments in anyone's career. I have resigned from my current role at a large bank where I have worked for 4 years. My manager came back with a counter-offer: a 22% raise and a promotion to team lead, which is exactly what I had been asking for for the past 18 months. My new offer is at a fintech startup — 15% higher base than my current (but lower than the counter), strong equity, but significantly more risk. Help me think through this decision clearly: (1) the statistical reality of counter-offers and why most people who accept them leave within a year, (2) a decision matrix framework for evaluating both options on 8 dimensions beyond money, (3) how to decline the counter-offer professionally if I choose the new role, and (4) how to accept the counter-offer without damaging my standing if I stay.","tags":["salary-negotiation","decision-making","career-growth"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-31","title":"Total Compensation Comparison Calculator Prompt","prompt":"You are a compensation analyst helping a mid-career professional compare two job offers that look similar on paper but are structurally very different. Offer A: $145,000 base, 15% annual bonus, 401k with 4% match, fully remote, no equity, 20 days PTO, strong healthcare. Offer B: $130,000 base, 0–20% discretionary bonus, 0.15% equity at $200M Series C valuation, hybrid 3 days in office in San Francisco, 4% 401k match, 15 days PTO, average healthcare. Build me a true total compensation comparison for both offers that accounts for: (1) annualized value of each component, (2) equity range across three valuation exit scenarios, (3) the dollar value of remote work vs. hybrid (commuting costs, time), (4) risk-adjusted expected value of the bonus components, and (5) a final side-by-side table with an honest assessment of which offer is better under what assumptions.","tags":["salary-negotiation","compensation","decision-making"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-32","title":"Negotiating in a Weak Market","prompt":"You are a salary negotiation coach who specializes in helping candidates maximize leverage even when they feel they have none — during economic downturns, layoff periods, or when they are unemployed. Many candidates believe they cannot negotiate if they are out of work or if the market is tight. I have been laid off for 4 months, I have one offer on the table for $95,000 for a data analyst role, and my previous salary was $115,000. I need the job but I also know I am being low-balled. Teach me: (1) how to negotiate with dignity when my leverage feels low, (2) the non-salary terms that are often flexible even when base pay is fixed, (3) the exact language to use that does not reveal my desperation, (4) how to use a 90-day performance review clause as a path to a faster raise, and (5) what to do if they reject my negotiation — how to accept gracefully while protecting future earning potential.","tags":["salary-negotiation","layoff","strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-33","title":"Raise Request Email That Gets Results","prompt":"You are an expert in written workplace communication. Most raise request emails are either too timid ('I was just wondering if maybe...') or too aggressive, and neither approach works. I want to send a written raise request email to my manager before our annual review cycle. I have been in my current role for 22 months, took on project management for a $2M client migration that I was not originally scoped for, received outstanding feedback in my last review, and have found market data suggesting I am paid 12% below market. Write a raise request email that: leads with the value I have delivered rather than my personal need, presents the market data as objective context rather than an ultimatum, makes a specific ask for a 14% increase to $92,000, keeps the tone collaborative and forward-looking, and sets up a meeting rather than demanding an answer by email. [Share any additional context about your situation or relationship with your manager]","tags":["salary-negotiation","email","workplace"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

    // --- CAREER PIVOTS (34-40) ---
    {"id":"car-34","title":"Skills Transferability Audit","prompt":"You are a career transition coach who has helped over 500 professionals successfully pivot industries. The most common mistake in a career pivot is believing you are starting from zero — most professionals dramatically underestimate how much of their existing skill set transfers directly. I am a 7-year Army officer transitioning to the private sector and targeting operations or consulting roles. Help me run a complete skills transferability audit: (1) take the core competencies of military leadership — mission planning, team development, crisis decision-making, logistics — and map each to specific private-sector equivalents with the correct business vocabulary, (2) identify which of my military experiences would be most compelling in a BCG or McKinsey case study context, (3) explain how to reframe 'I commanded 120 soldiers' for a corporate audience without sounding like I am translating a foreign language, and (4) identify the 2–3 genuine skill gaps I need to close before being competitive.","tags":["career-pivot","military","transferable-skills"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-35","title":"Industry Pivot Research Plan","prompt":"You are a career strategist who designs research plans for professionals seriously considering an industry change. Most people research a new industry the wrong way — they read articles, consume podcasts, and form opinions from the outside. Real intelligence comes from primary research. I am a B2B enterprise sales professional considering a pivot into climate tech. I know almost nothing about the industry beyond headlines. Design a 6-week industry immersion plan that includes: (1) week-by-week activities across reading, listening, and talking to people, (2) a shortlist of the 10 most important voices to follow in climate tech and why, (3) a script for reaching out to 3–5 climate tech professionals for 20-minute informational interviews, (4) the 10 questions I should ask in every informational interview to accelerate my learning, and (5) how to convert this research into content and conversations that build my credibility in the new space before I have any experience.","tags":["career-pivot","research","networking"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-36","title":"Back to Work After a Career Break","prompt":"You are a returnship strategist and career coach who specializes in helping professionals re-enter the workforce after a significant break. I left my career as a software engineer 3 years ago to raise my children. I am now ready to return to work. The tech landscape has changed significantly — there are new frameworks, AI tools have reshaped many roles, and I am worried I will be dismissed by companies as 'rusty.' Build me a 12-week return-to-work plan that includes: (1) the fastest path to closing the technical skill gap without a full-time bootcamp, (2) how to address the gap confidently in interviews and on my resume, (3) which types of companies and roles are most returnship-friendly, (4) how to rebuild my professional network after 3 years of minimal activity, and (5) how to evaluate whether a returnship program is right for me vs. going direct.","tags":["career-pivot","career-gap","returnship"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-37","title":"From IC to Manager: Is It Right for You?","prompt":"You are an executive coach who has worked with hundreds of professionals navigating the individual contributor to manager transition. This is one of the most consequential career decisions a professional can make — and it is frequently made for the wrong reasons, because management is the only visible path to advancement. I am a senior software engineer who has been offered my first engineering manager role. I am genuinely unsure whether I want it. Help me think through this decision rigorously: (1) the honest realities of what changes when you become a manager — both what you gain and what you permanently give up, (2) a set of 10 self-assessment questions that distinguish people who thrive as managers from those who are miserable, (3) how to take the role as an experiment with a defined evaluation period rather than a permanent identity shift, and (4) how to build in an 'offramp' back to IC work if the role is not right for you.","tags":["career-pivot","management","decision-making"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-38","title":"Functional Role Switch Within Your Company","prompt":"You are an internal career coach. Many professionals do not realize that the most accessible career pivot is often within their current employer — moving from one function to another without changing companies. I am a finance analyst at a large tech company who wants to move into a business operations or strategy role. I have been here 3 years, have a strong reputation, but have never formally expressed this interest. Create a 90-day internal pivot campaign: (1) how to signal my interest to the right people without creating anxiety about my loyalty, (2) how to start doing the work of the new role before I have the title, (3) how to identify and approach a champion or sponsor who can advocate for my move, (4) how to make the formal ask once I have built the right conditions, and (5) what to do if my manager blocks the transfer.","tags":["career-pivot","internal-mobility","strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-39","title":"Starting Over at 40+ Career Reinvention Guide","prompt":"You are a career reinvention coach who specializes in working with professionals in their 40s and 50s who are ready for a major change. Conventional career advice is written for 25-year-olds. At 40+, the calculus is different — you have real financial obligations, a deeper network, more self-knowledge, but also more to lose and less patience for wasted time. I am 47, have spent 20 years in pharmaceutical sales, and want to transition into healthcare consulting or health tech. I do not want to start at the bottom. Design a reinvention strategy that: (1) identifies the seniority level I should target given my experience (not entry-level, even if I am new to the sector), (2) shows how to position 20 years of industry-specific relationships as a premium asset rather than a liability, (3) maps out a realistic 12–18 month transition timeline with milestones, and (4) honestly addresses the age bias I may encounter and specific tactics to mitigate it.","tags":["career-pivot","reinvention","mid-career"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true},
    {"id":"car-40","title":"Pivot Bridge Plan: Keep Income While Building","prompt":"You are a career strategist who specializes in low-risk career pivots. The biggest barrier to pivoting is financial — most people cannot afford to quit and retrain full-time. I want to pivot from my current role as a high school teacher into instructional design for corporate learning and development. I have 8 years of teaching experience, a strong understanding of curriculum design, and have used Articulate 360 on one personal project. I need to keep my full-time income while building toward the new career. Design a 6-month parallel-track plan: (1) the 3–5 skills I need to develop to be competitive (and the fastest legitimate ways to develop them), (2) how to build a portfolio of 2–3 instructional design samples on weekends, (3) how to find freelance or volunteer L&D projects to build real-world experience, (4) when and how to start applying for full-time L&D roles, and (5) how to handle the income transition.","tags":["career-pivot","freelancing","planning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

    // --- PERSONAL BRANDING & LINKEDIN (41-52) ---
    {"id":"car-41","title":"LinkedIn Profile Complete Overhaul","prompt":"You are a LinkedIn optimization expert and personal branding coach whose clients have used LinkedIn to land board roles, book speaking gigs, and attract inbound recruiter opportunities. Most LinkedIn profiles are passive — they read like a digital resume instead of a dynamic brand asset. I am a senior cybersecurity consultant with 10 years of experience, currently at Deloitte, targeting CISO roles at mid-size financial services firms within the next 2 years. Rewrite or give me the exact framework to rewrite: (1) my LinkedIn headline — make it 3 variants (keyword-rich, bold claim, and hybrid), (2) my About section — 4–5 paragraphs that tell my story, establish authority, and include a clear call to action, (3) my featured section — what to put there and why, and (4) my experience section — how many bullet points per role and what should be in each. [Paste your current LinkedIn profile text here]","tags":["linkedin","personal-branding","job-search"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true},
    {"id":"car-42","title":"LinkedIn Content Strategy for Visibility","prompt":"You are a LinkedIn content strategist whose clients have grown from zero followers to 20,000+ engaged professionals by posting consistently and strategically. I am a supply chain director at a Fortune 500 consumer goods company. I want to build a LinkedIn presence that positions me as a thought leader in supply chain resilience and nearshoring. I have zero content experience, 400 connections, and about 2 hours per week to invest. Build me a 90-day content strategy: (1) the 4 content pillars I should own, (2) the ideal posting frequency and the best time to post for supply chain professionals, (3) five post formats I should rotate between with examples of each, (4) how to write a LinkedIn post that actually gets engagement vs. gets ignored, and (5) the single most important thing to do in the first 5 minutes after posting to maximize reach.","tags":["linkedin","content","personal-branding"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-43","title":"LinkedIn Headline Generator — 10 Options","prompt":"You are a LinkedIn copywriter who has A/B tested hundreds of headlines. The LinkedIn headline is the highest-leverage piece of text on your profile — it appears in search results, under your name in comments, and is the first thing a recruiter reads. Most headlines waste this space with a job title and company name, which adds zero information beyond what is already visible. I need 10 compelling LinkedIn headline options for a freelance UX designer with 6 years of experience specializing in fintech and healthcare apps, who wants to attract both inbound client inquiries and full-time job opportunities. For each headline, give me: (1) the headline text (under 220 characters), (2) the strategy it uses (keyword-rich, bold claim, problem-solution, social proof, etc.), and (3) the scenario in which it works best.","tags":["linkedin","personal-branding","copywriting"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-44","title":"Personal Brand Clarity Session","prompt":"You are a personal branding strategist running a clarity session with a professional who knows they need a stronger brand but does not know where to start. Before you can build a personal brand, you need to be clear on three things: what you stand for, who you are serving, and what makes your perspective unique. Run a structured brand discovery session with me. Ask me 8 questions — one at a time, waiting for my response — designed to uncover: my core expertise and where it intersects with passion, the audience I most want to reach and what they need from me, the things I believe about my field that most people in it get wrong, and the one topic I could talk about for 4 hours without notes. After I answer all 8 questions, synthesize my answers into a one-paragraph personal brand statement and a brand platform with 3 content pillars. [Type 'Ready' to begin the session]","tags":["personal-branding","clarity","strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-45","title":"Executive Bio for Speaking, Boards, and Press","prompt":"You are a communications consultant who writes bios for C-suite executives, keynote speakers, and board members. A good executive bio has to do multiple jobs simultaneously: establish authority quickly, feel human and not robotic, work in a 75-word short version and a 250-word full version, and be updatable. I am a Chief Revenue Officer at a Series D healthcare AI company, previously VP of Sales at Salesforce and a startup that was acquired by Oracle in 2019. I am building a board portfolio and have two speaking engagements coming up. Write three versions of my bio: (1) a 50-word version for conference programs, (2) a 150-word version for speaking bios and board applications, and (3) a 300-word version for press features and company website. Each should feel authoritative without being pompous and include a personal detail that makes me human. [Provide your specific career details and any personal details you want included]","tags":["personal-branding","bio","executive"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-46","title":"Thought Leadership Article Outline","prompt":"You are a ghostwriter and content strategist who writes thought leadership articles for senior executives published in Harvard Business Review, Forbes, and Fast Company. A strong thought leadership article does not summarize conventional wisdom — it takes a counterintuitive stance, backs it with specific evidence, and changes how the reader thinks about something they thought they understood. I want to write an article for LinkedIn or a trade publication on the topic: why most diversity and inclusion programs at tech companies fail and what actually works, based on 12 years of HR leadership experience. Produce: (1) three possible article angles with a specific thesis statement for each, (2) a full outline for the strongest angle with 5–6 sections and the key point of each, (3) the opening hook sentence for each of the three angles, and (4) advice on how to handle the political sensitivity of this topic while still saying something real.","tags":["personal-branding","writing","thought-leadership"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-47","title":"Professional Headshot Brief for Photographers","prompt":"You are a personal brand consultant who prepares executives for professional photo shoots. A professional headshot is one of the most important personal branding assets you have — it communicates competence, approachability, and personality before you say a word. Most people show up to a photoshoot with no brief and get mediocre results. I am a 42-year-old Chief Marketing Officer preparing for new headshots to use on LinkedIn, my company website, and speaking engagements. I want to project: warmth and approachability, credibility without stuffiness, and a hint of creative energy appropriate for a marketing leader. Write a photographer brief covering: (1) the three background settings to request and why each works for different platforms, (2) clothing recommendations and what to avoid, (3) the expression and body language to aim for (specific and coachable, not 'look friendly'), and (4) the shot list — how many looks, angles, and types of shots to request.","tags":["personal-branding","headshot","visual-identity"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-48","title":"Reputation Audit: What Does Google Say About You?","prompt":"You are a personal branding strategist specializing in online reputation management. Your digital footprint is the first thing a recruiter, board member, or potential client will check — often before they even reach out. Most professionals have never deliberately managed what appears when their name is searched. Walk me through a comprehensive personal reputation audit: (1) the exact search queries to run and what I should be looking for in the results, (2) how to interpret what your absence from Google says to decision-makers, (3) the priority order for building positive search results (LinkedIn, articles, company bios, podcasts, etc.), (4) how to handle negative results — old articles, controversial social media posts, or a namesake with unflattering content, and (5) a 60-day action plan to shift my Google first-page results from passive to actively reinforcing my professional brand.","tags":["personal-branding","reputation","digital"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-49","title":"LinkedIn Recommendations: Give and Request","prompt":"You are a LinkedIn optimization expert. LinkedIn recommendations are the most underleveraged trust signals on the platform — a strong recommendation from the right person can be more persuasive than anything you write about yourself. But most recommendations are useless: generic praise that says nothing specific and could apply to anyone. Help me on two fronts: (1) how to request a recommendation in a way that makes it easy for the recommender and dramatically increases the quality of what they write — give me the exact email template, (2) how to write a powerful recommendation for a colleague that will actually get read and believed — give me a structure and a full example for a product designer I worked closely with for 2 years, and (3) which recommendations I should prioritize getting (by role, relationship, and recency) to maximize their signal value for my next career move. [Describe your target role and the relationship with the person you want a recommendation from]","tags":["linkedin","networking","personal-branding"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-50","title":"Speaking Proposal for Industry Conferences","prompt":"You are a speaking bureau consultant who helps professionals get selected to speak at their industry's top conferences. Most speaking submissions are rejected because they are too generic, sound like a company pitch, or do not give the selection committee a clear picture of what the audience will walk away with. I am a VP of Customer Success at a SaaS company who wants to build a speaking career. My proposed talk topic: how to reduce churn by redesigning the customer onboarding experience based on behavioral data. I have an 8-minute recorded talk at a company all-hands, but no conference speaking credits. Write a complete speaking proposal including: (1) a conference-ready talk title with three options, (2) a 150-word abstract that sells both the speaker and the content, (3) five specific audience takeaways in punchy bullet form, (4) a 3-sentence speaker bio for the proposal, and (5) advice on which conferences to target first as a new speaker.","tags":["personal-branding","speaking","content"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-51","title":"Podcast Guest Pitch That Gets Booked","prompt":"You are a media relations consultant who specializes in helping executives and thought leaders get booked on podcasts. Podcast appearances are one of the most efficient personal branding tools available — one appearance can reach thousands of your ideal audience and lives permanently as searchable content. I am a behavioral economist who consults for fintech companies on decision architecture. I want to pitch myself as a guest to 10–15 podcasts in the fintech, product, and behavioral science space. Write: (1) a master pitch email template I can personalize for each show (under 150 words), (2) three distinct talk track options I can pitch — each with a title, a 2-sentence premise, and 3 bullet points of what listeners will learn, (3) how to research a podcast before pitching to avoid obvious mistakes, and (4) how to follow up professionally if I do not hear back in 2 weeks.","tags":["personal-branding","podcasting","outreach"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-52","title":"Online Portfolio Case Study Builder","prompt":"You are a portfolio strategist who works with UX designers, consultants, product managers, and marketers to build case studies that win clients and job offers. Most portfolio case studies are process dumps — they show every step the professional took without making the reader care about the outcome. A compelling case study has a clear problem, a protagonist (you), a decision moment, and an outcome the reader envies. I am a management consultant who wants to build three case studies for my personal website to attract future clients. The first project: I redesigned the customer journey for a regional bank, reducing time-to-close for a new account from 11 days to 3 days. Help me write the complete case study in a web-ready format with: headline, 1-sentence problem statement, your role, the 3-step approach, the outcome with metrics, and a client quote placeholder. Then give me the template I can use for the other two. [Describe your project details here]","tags":["personal-branding","portfolio","consulting"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

    // --- NETWORKING (53-60) ---
    {"id":"car-53","title":"Network Mapping and Prioritization System","prompt":"You are a networking strategist who believes that most professionals have a far richer network than they realize — they simply have not organized it intentionally. The goal is not to have the most connections, but to have the right relationships at the right depth. Help me design a personal network mapping system from scratch. Walk me through: (1) how to do a complete network audit — where to look (LinkedIn, email, old colleagues, school contacts), (2) a tiering system (A, B, C) based on relationship strength and strategic relevance, (3) a minimum viable maintenance plan for each tier — how often to reach out and what kind of touchpoints keep a relationship warm, (4) the most common networking mistakes smart people make (transactional timing, only reaching out when they need something), and (5) a 30-day plan to reconnect with 10 dormant relationships without it feeling awkward.","tags":["networking","strategy","relationships"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-54","title":"Informational Interview Mastery Guide","prompt":"You are a networking coach specializing in informational interviews — one of the most powerful and underused tools in a professional's career toolkit. An informational interview done well opens doors; done poorly, it wastes everyone's time and burns a relationship. I want to conduct 8 informational interviews with professionals in the venture capital industry over the next 6 weeks. I currently work in corporate finance and am exploring a move into VC. Write me: (1) the outreach message that gets a 'yes' response — two versions, cold outreach and warm intro, (2) a structured agenda for the 25-minute call with opening, 5 core questions, and closing, (3) the 10 best questions to ask a VC professional that show I have done my homework and think deeply, (4) what to do in the 24 hours after the call to leave a lasting impression, and (5) how to convert an informational interview into an ongoing relationship rather than a one-time transaction.","tags":["networking","informational-interview","career-pivot"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-55","title":"Conference Networking Prep Protocol","prompt":"You are a professional networking coach who prepares executives and professionals for high-value industry conferences. Most people attend conferences and come home with a pile of business cards and zero new relationships. Effective conference networking is a skill — it requires preparation, intentionality, and a clear follow-up system. I am attending a 3-day marketing technology conference next month with approximately 2,000 attendees. I want to leave with 5 meaningful new relationships that could lead to business partnerships, a speaking opportunity, or a job referral within 12 months. Design my complete conference strategy: (1) the pre-conference research and outreach plan (who to target, what to say), (2) the conversational opening move that is not 'So what do you do?', (3) how to gracefully exit conversations to maximize the number of people I meet, (4) what to capture in my notes immediately after each conversation, and (5) the follow-up sequence for the 7 days post-conference.","tags":["networking","conferences","strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-56","title":"Building a Mentorship Relationship","prompt":"You are a mentorship strategist who has studied the structures of the most effective professional mentoring relationships. Most mentorship relationships fail because they are too vague — the mentee shows up without an agenda, the mentor gives generic advice, and the relationship fizzles after 3–4 sessions. I have identified someone I deeply respect — a 55-year-old Chief People Officer at a public tech company — who I would like to ask to be my mentor. I am a 29-year-old HR business partner. Write me: (1) the outreach message to ask her to be my mentor — specific, humble, and time-bounded, (2) a proposed structure for a 6-month mentorship engagement including frequency, duration, and agenda format, (3) how to prepare for every meeting to make the best use of her time, (4) what constitutes a great mentee (because mentors invest in those who take their advice seriously), and (5) how to evolve the relationship from formal mentorship to a peer advisory friendship over time.","tags":["networking","mentorship","career-growth"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-57","title":"LinkedIn Connection Request Message Library","prompt":"You are a LinkedIn copywriter and networking expert. The default 'I'd like to connect' message on LinkedIn is a missed opportunity — a brief, personalized note dramatically increases acceptance rates and sets the tone for a meaningful relationship. Write me a library of 10 LinkedIn connection request messages for different scenarios, each under 300 characters: (1) after meeting at a conference, (2) cold outreach to a peer at a target company, (3) to a recruiter who viewed my profile, (4) to a thought leader whose post I found genuinely insightful, (5) to a former colleague I lost touch with, (6) to a hiring manager at a company I am targeting, (7) to a speaker after a webinar, (8) to a potential mentor, (9) to an alum from my school I have never met, and (10) to someone I have been following for a long time. For each, explain the tactic and when to use it.","tags":["linkedin","networking","copywriting"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-58","title":"Give-Before-You-Ask Networking Framework","prompt":"You are a relationship-building strategist who teaches the 'give first' approach to professional networking. The most common networking mistake is asking for something before establishing value. The best networkers are known as connectors, sharers, and helpers — and that reputation makes people want to return the favor. I want to build genuine relationships with 20 professionals in the data engineering community over the next 90 days. I have no existing relationships in this space. Design a 90-day give-first networking campaign: (1) five ways to add value to someone in data engineering before asking for anything, (2) how to use content (shares, comments, articles) as a relationship-building tool, (3) how to make introductions between other people in a way that builds your own reputation, (4) the transition point — when and how to make an ask after you have established value, and (5) how to track the health of your network investments without feeling mercenary.","tags":["networking","strategy","relationships"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-59","title":"Alumni Network Activation Plan","prompt":"You are a career coach who specializes in helping professionals unlock the underused power of their alumni network. Alumni connections are among the highest-converting professional relationships because of shared identity — but most people never use them strategically. I graduated from a target MBA program 6 years ago and have barely engaged with the alumni network since. I am now targeting a move into private equity and know the alumni network has former PE professionals who might help. Help me design a 60-day alumni network activation campaign: (1) how to find the right alumni in PE through the alumni portal and LinkedIn, (2) the outreach message that leverages the alumni bond without being cliché, (3) the right ask that respects their time (not 'Can you help me get a job?'), (4) how to use the career center resources I am technically still entitled to as an alum, and (5) how to give back to the network in a way that builds goodwill and visibility simultaneously.","tags":["networking","alumni","career-pivot"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-60","title":"Building a Personal Advisory Board","prompt":"You are an executive coach who helps professionals build their own Personal Board of Advisors — a small, intentional group of 4–6 advisors who provide different types of guidance, challenge, and connection. Having a formal advisory board is one of the most effective career accelerators for ambitious professionals, but few people below C-suite level think to create one. I am a 35-year-old VP of Engineering at a fintech company who wants to eventually become a CTO. Help me design my personal advisory board: (1) the 5 archetypes I should have on my board and what each contributes (technical mentor, industry connector, challenging critic, career sponsor, life wisdom figure), (2) how to evaluate who in my current network fits each role, (3) how to ask someone to be on your personal advisory board without it sounding weird or presumptuous, (4) how to structure quarterly board meetings or touchpoints, and (5) how to keep the relationships reciprocal so advisors stay engaged.","tags":["networking","mentorship","career-planning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

    // --- CAREER PLANNING & PROMOTIONS (61-72) ---
    {"id":"car-61","title":"5-Year Career Vision and Roadmap","prompt":"You are a career architect who helps ambitious professionals build deliberate, non-linear career paths rather than drifting from role to role. Most professionals do their career planning by reacting to opportunities rather than proactively designing their trajectory. I am 31 years old, currently a Senior Brand Manager at a CPG company, and I want to be a Chief Marketing Officer at a mid-size consumer brand by the time I am 40. Help me build a rigorous 5-year career architecture: (1) the specific roles and experiences I need on my resume before I am a credible CMO candidate, (2) the skills and credentials that would accelerate my path vs. the ones that are optional, (3) the types of companies I should target at each stage and why, (4) a year-by-year milestone map with leading indicators I can track, and (5) the hardest honest question I need to answer about whether this goal is right for me.","tags":["career-planning","strategy","goal-setting"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-62","title":"Promotion Case Builder","prompt":"You are a career coach specializing in the internal promotion process. Getting promoted requires more than doing excellent work — it requires making your work visible, building sponsorship, and timing your ask correctly. I have been in my current role as a Senior Software Engineer for 2 years and am ready to make the case for Principal Engineer. My most significant contributions: redesigned the data ingestion pipeline reducing latency by 62%, mentored 3 junior engineers who all received positive performance reviews, and initiated and led the migration to our new CI/CD system. Help me build a formal promotion case: (1) a one-page written document I can share with my manager and skip-level, (2) the quantified impact of each contribution framed at a Principal Engineer level, (3) evidence I am already operating at the next level, (4) how to address the 'not yet' response proactively, and (5) how to involve my manager as a co-sponsor rather than gatekeeper.","tags":["promotion","career-planning","strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true},
    {"id":"car-63","title":"Performance Review Self-Assessment Rewriter","prompt":"You are a career coach and organizational psychologist who has seen thousands of performance review self-assessments. Most self-assessments are either too humble (underselling genuine impact) or too vague (listing activities without outcomes). Strong self-assessments tell a clear performance story: what you were asked to do, what you actually achieved, and why it mattered to the business. Rewrite my performance review self-assessment using the following principles: lead each section with an outcome, not an activity; replace every instance of 'I worked on' with a verb that shows ownership; quantify every claim that can be quantified; and add a forward-looking paragraph that ties this year's performance to next year's ambitions. [Paste your current self-assessment draft here]","tags":["promotion","performance","writing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-64","title":"Visibility Strategy for Overlooked High Performers","prompt":"You are a career strategist who specializes in helping high performers who are passed over for promotion because they are doing excellent work invisibly. This is one of the most frustrating career situations: working harder than anyone on the team, getting great feedback, and watching less-talented colleagues get promoted because they play the visibility game better. I am a lead data analyst who has been passed over for a senior manager role twice despite strong performance reviews. I am introverted and uncomfortable with self-promotion. Build me an ethical, authentic visibility strategy: (1) the difference between bragging and strategic visibility (and how to stay on the right side of the line), (2) five specific tactics that increase visibility in meetings, presentations, and Slack/email without feeling performative, (3) how to make my manager's success depend partially on my own work, and (4) how to build relationships with senior leaders through value, not schmoozing.","tags":["promotion","career-planning","visibility"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-65","title":"30-60-90 Day Plan for a New Role","prompt":"You are an executive onboarding coach who has designed first-90-day plans for VPs and Directors at hundreds of companies. The first 90 days in a new senior role are the most important — they set your reputation, establish your operating style, and either build or destroy the political capital you need to execute. I am joining a new company next month as Director of Customer Experience at a 350-person Series C startup. I will be leading a team of 9, inheriting a backlog of customer complaints, and reporting to the COO. Build me a detailed 30-60-90 day plan: Phase 1 (Listen and Learn) — the 15 people I should meet and what to ask each, how to diagnose the team's health, and what NOT to change yet. Phase 2 (Assess and Plan) — how to produce a diagnosis memo the COO will actually read. Phase 3 (Act) — how to sequence my first initiatives for maximum early wins and long-term impact.","tags":["career-planning","onboarding","leadership"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true},
    {"id":"car-66","title":"Managing Up: Working with a Difficult Boss","prompt":"You are an organizational coach specializing in upward management — the art of navigating a complex or challenging relationship with your direct manager. Most career advice focuses on leading downward. But your relationship with your manager is the single biggest variable in your career growth. I have a new manager who is a micromanager — she reviews every deliverable I produce before it goes out, asks me to CC her on emails I send to other teams, and contradicts my decisions in front of my team. I want to address this directly without damaging the relationship. Give me: (1) a diagnostic framework to understand whether this is a style issue or a trust issue, (2) the opening conversation to have with her — exact language, not a confrontation, (3) how to proactively give her the visibility she needs so she feels confident enough to back off, (4) how to set a boundary when she undermines me publicly, and (5) when to escalate to HR vs. handling it directly.","tags":["career-planning","management","workplace"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-67","title":"Annual Career Review Ritual","prompt":"You are a career coach who believes that the most successful professionals treat their career as a portfolio to be actively managed, not a river to be floated down. An annual career review is the most important ritual a professional can build — more important than any single job move. Guide me through a comprehensive annual career review process. I want to do this on my birthday each year, spending 3–4 hours in honest reflection and planning. Build me a complete review template with: (1) a backward-looking section: this year's wins, failures, surprises, and relationships built, (2) a gap analysis between where I am and where I said I wanted to be 12 months ago, (3) a values and energy audit — what energized me and what drained me this year, (4) a forward-looking section: my 3 most important career goals for the next 12 months with specific metrics, and (5) a letter I write to myself to open next year.","tags":["career-planning","self-reflection","goal-setting"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-68","title":"Handling a PIP (Performance Improvement Plan)","prompt":"You are a career coach and employment advocate with 20 years of experience helping professionals navigate Performance Improvement Plans. A PIP can feel like the end of the road, but it is often the beginning of a decision point — either turn it around and stay, or use the time to exit on your own terms. I just received a PIP at my current company. It outlines 4 areas of concern over a 60-day period with specific metrics I need to hit. I genuinely believe some of the feedback is valid and some is political. Help me think through this clearly: (1) the honest first question: is this a fair PIP or a managed exit? How to tell the difference, (2) if I want to fight and stay — the exact actions to take in the first 14 days to signal seriousness, (3) if I decide to use this time to job search — how to do so confidentially without violating my obligations, (4) how to negotiate the terms of the PIP if they are unreasonable, and (5) how to document everything to protect myself.","tags":["career-planning","workplace","crisis"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-69","title":"Layoff Survival and Recovery Plan","prompt":"You are a career transition coach specializing in helping professionals navigate layoffs with clarity and speed. Being laid off is one of the most disorienting professional experiences — even when it is entirely macro-economic and has nothing to do with performance. I was laid off yesterday from my role as a senior product manager at a tech company. I had 6 years there. I am in shock, I have about 8 months of runway financially, and I do not know where to start. Walk me through the first 30 days: (1) the first 48 hours — the practical and emotional tasks in order of priority, (2) how to negotiate my severance before I sign anything, (3) how to file for unemployment correctly and what to say, (4) how to tell my network and reframe the narrative so it does not feel like asking for help from a position of weakness, (5) the job search strategy that gives me the highest probability of a strong landing within 90 days.","tags":["layoff","job-search","career-planning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true},
    {"id":"car-70","title":"Career Decision Framework for Complex Crossroads","prompt":"You are an executive career coach running a decision-making session with a professional facing a genuinely complex career crossroad. I am 36, a senior product manager at a major tech company earning $210k total comp, and I have three options on the table simultaneously: (1) a Director-level internal promotion that would be a step up with more visibility but less autonomy, (2) a VP of Product offer at a Series A startup at $165k base plus meaningful equity, and (3) staying in my current role and focusing on building a side advisory practice for 12–18 months before making a move. Run me through a structured decision-making framework that: (1) helps me identify what I actually value vs. what I think I should value, (2) stress-tests each option against a 5-year version of my career, (3) surfaces the fears and assumptions I am bringing to this decision that I may not be aware of, and (4) leads me to a clear recommendation with confidence.","tags":["career-planning","decision-making","strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-71","title":"Skills Gap Analysis for Your Next Role","prompt":"You are a career development coach who runs skill gap analyses for professionals targeting a specific next role. Most professionals either over-prepare (getting another degree when experience would suffice) or under-prepare (applying for roles they are genuinely not ready for). I am a customer success manager with 4 years of experience and want to move into product management at a B2B SaaS company within 18 months. I have never held a PM title. Run a skills gap analysis for me: (1) the core competencies of a strong entry-level PM at a B2B SaaS company, ranked by importance, (2) an honest assessment of which skills I likely have from customer success that transfer directly, (3) the 3–4 gaps I genuinely need to close and the fastest legitimate way to close each one, (4) the experiences I should seek in my current role to build PM-adjacent credibility, and (5) the portfolio of work I should be able to show by the time I apply.","tags":["career-planning","skills","career-pivot"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-72","title":"Relocation Career Decision Framework","prompt":"You are a career strategist and life design coach helping a professional navigate a relocation decision that intersects deeply with career trajectory. Relocation decisions are rarely purely career decisions — they involve a life partner, lifestyle preferences, financial implications, and career opportunity costs that are hard to calculate. My company is offering me a significant promotion — a jump from Senior Manager to Director, with a 28% raise — but it requires relocating from Austin to New York City. My partner works remotely so the relocation is possible but costly, and I have strong reservations about cost of living. Help me think through this decision with rigor: (1) the true financial comparison after COL adjustment, taxes, and housing, (2) the career trajectory value of the NY opportunity vs. staying in Austin and waiting for the next opening, (3) a framework for having the conversation with my partner as a joint life decision rather than a work ultimatum, and (4) the questions I should ask my employer about the role before accepting.","tags":["career-planning","relocation","decision-making"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

    // --- FREELANCING & ENTREPRENEURSHIP (73-80) ---
    {"id":"car-73","title":"Freelance Rate Calculator and Pitch","prompt":"You are a freelance business coach who has helped hundreds of consultants price their services profitably. Most freelancers underprice themselves chronically — they set rates based on what feels comfortable to charge rather than what the market will bear or what they need to earn. I am a freelance UX designer in Chicago with 7 years of experience, specializing in fintech product design. I want to charge $150/hour but have been charging $95/hour because I am afraid of losing clients. Help me: (1) calculate whether $150/hour is actually reasonable given my market and experience, (2) identify the specific value signals I need to demonstrate to justify the premium rate, (3) script the rate increase conversation with my three existing retainer clients, (4) reframe how I present my rate to new clients so the conversation is about value, not cost, and (5) write the 'I'm raising my rates' email that retains client relationships.","tags":["freelancing","pricing","business"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-74","title":"Freelance Proposal That Wins Projects","prompt":"You are a freelance business development coach who has reviewed thousands of proposals. The difference between a proposal that wins and one that loses is almost never price — it is how clearly the freelancer demonstrates they understand the client's actual problem and how confident they are that they can solve it. I am a freelance content strategist pitching a 3-month project to a mid-size B2B software company to overhaul their blog and content marketing function. The budget range is $12,000–$18,000. Write me a proposal template that: (1) opens with the client's problem in their own language (not my service offering), (2) presents three tiers of engagement at different price points, (3) includes a clear scope of work with deliverables and a timeline, (4) addresses the risk question ('What if this does not work?') proactively, and (5) closes with a specific next step rather than 'let me know what you think.' [Paste any notes from your discovery call with the client]","tags":["freelancing","proposals","business-development"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-75","title":"From Employee to Solopreneur Transition Plan","prompt":"You are a business coach who specializes in helping employed professionals transition into independent consulting or freelancing. The leap from employment to self-employment is one of the most exciting and most poorly planned career moves professionals make. I am a 10-year marketing consultant at a Big 4 firm who wants to go independent within 12 months. I have $60,000 in savings, one potential anchor client (a former colleague who said 'I would definitely hire you'), and zero business infrastructure. Build me a pre-launch checklist and 12-month transition plan: (1) the financial runway calculation and minimum viable monthly income target, (2) the business and legal setup I actually need in month 1 (vs. what I am overthinking), (3) how to validate the business model before I quit, (4) how to build a pipeline of clients before day one of independence, and (5) the moment I will know it is safe to quit.","tags":["freelancing","entrepreneurship","planning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-76","title":"Consulting Services Productization Framework","prompt":"You are a business strategist who helps service professionals transform their expertise into productized offerings — defined, repeatable deliverables with a fixed price and timeline rather than open-ended hourly engagements. Productized services are easier to sell, easier to deliver, and create more predictable revenue. I am a freelance HR consultant specializing in culture assessments and manager effectiveness. Currently all my work is bespoke and hourly. Help me design three productized service offerings from my expertise: (1) a diagnostic product (a one-time assessment), (2) a transformation product (a structured program over 60–90 days), and (3) a retainer product (ongoing advisory access). For each: give it a compelling name, define the deliverables, suggest a price range, write a one-paragraph sales description, and identify the ideal buyer persona.","tags":["freelancing","productization","business"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-77","title":"Client Testimonial and Case Study Extraction","prompt":"You are a marketing consultant who helps service providers turn client results into powerful testimonials and case studies. Strong social proof is the most effective marketing tool for a freelancer or consultant, but most professionals either forget to ask for testimonials or receive ones that are too generic to be persuasive ('They did great work and I would recommend them!'). I need to collect better testimonials from my 8 existing clients and turn my best two client engagements into case studies. Write me: (1) a post-project survey with 5 questions designed to pull out specific, quotable testimonials rather than vague praise, (2) an email asking for a testimonial from a client with whom the project wrapped up 3 months ago, (3) a framework for writing a case study with the following sections: client challenge, approach, results, and a client quote, and (4) where and how to use these assets to convert new client inquiries.","tags":["freelancing","marketing","social-proof"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-78","title":"Freelance Contract Essentials Guide","prompt":"You are a freelance business advisor (not a lawyer) who helps independent professionals understand the business essentials of protecting their work. Most freelancers use inadequate contracts — or no contract at all — until they have a bad experience. I am a freelance web developer who has mostly worked on handshake agreements. I recently had a client dispute over scope and am now serious about protecting myself. Walk me through the 10 clauses every freelance contract should include: payment terms (deposit, milestone, final), scope of work and what constitutes a change request, kill fee clause, IP ownership and licensing, confidentiality, revision rounds, cancellation terms, late payment penalties, dispute resolution, and limitation of liability. For each clause, explain what it protects me from and give me a plain-language template sentence I can adapt.","tags":["freelancing","contracts","legal"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-79","title":"Side Project to Revenue in 90 Days","prompt":"You are a business coach specializing in helping professionals monetize their expertise and side projects quickly. I am a product manager with 8 years of experience who has been asked repeatedly by friends and LinkedIn followers for advice on PM career transitions. I have been thinking about creating a paid offering but have done nothing about it. I want to generate my first $5,000 from this expertise within 90 days without quitting my job. Build me a 90-day monetization sprint: (1) the fastest path to first revenue — coaching, workshop, template, or course — and why one approach makes more sense for my situation, (2) how to validate there is genuine willingness-to-pay before I build anything, (3) the minimum viable product I can launch in 30 days, (4) how to use my existing audience to drive the first 10 paying customers, and (5) the pricing and positioning strategy that feels right and is financially intelligent.","tags":["freelancing","entrepreneurship","side-project"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-80","title":"Difficult Client Situation Handling Scripts","prompt":"You are a freelance business coach specializing in client management and conflict resolution. Every freelancer eventually faces these situations: a scope creep conversation, a client who is chronically late on payments, a client asking you to work for exposure, and a relationship that has turned toxic. Most freelancers handle these situations either too passively (and get walked on) or too aggressively (and lose the client or their reputation). Write me four short, professional scripts for the following situations: (1) a client who keeps adding scope without paying for it ('just one more small thing'), (2) a client who is 45 days late on a $4,500 invoice, (3) a startup founder asking me to work for equity instead of cash, and (4) a client whose requests have become disrespectful and I need to either reset the relationship or exit it gracefully. For each script, give me the opening line, the core message, and the proposed outcome.","tags":["freelancing","client-management","communication"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

    // --- LEADERSHIP DEVELOPMENT & EXECUTIVE PRESENCE (81-92) ---
    {"id":"car-81","title":"Executive Presence: The Complete Framework","prompt":"You are an executive presence coach who has worked with senior leaders at McKinsey, Google, and the Fortune 50. Executive presence is the most talked about and least well-defined leadership competency — described as a feeling people get, but rarely broken down into learnable components. I have received feedback in my 360 review that I need to 'develop more executive presence' before I can be promoted to VP. I am a strong analytical performer but tend to be soft-spoken in large meetings and struggle to project confidence when challenged. Break down executive presence into its concrete, trainable components: (1) the three dimensions — gravitas, communication, and appearance — and what each actually means in practice, (2) the specific behaviors that signal low executive presence in meetings (and their high-presence alternatives), (3) a 60-day development plan with daily and weekly practices, and (4) how to project confidence when you are genuinely uncertain.","tags":["executive-presence","leadership","communication"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true},
    {"id":"car-82","title":"Leading Through Organizational Change","prompt":"You are an organizational change expert and leadership coach. Leading a team through significant change — a restructure, a merger, a pivot, or a reduction in force — is one of the highest-stakes moments in a manager's career. Done poorly, you lose your best people and your credibility. Done well, you emerge as a leader who can be trusted in a crisis. I am a VP of Engineering who has just been told I need to lead a 20% team reduction affecting 12 engineers next quarter. I have never done this before. Walk me through the entire process: (1) how to communicate the news to my team — timing, format, and exact words, (2) how to make the selection decisions fairly and document them appropriately, (3) how to support the people who are leaving with dignity and generosity, (4) how to rebuild morale and trust with the people who remain (the 'survivor syndrome' problem), and (5) how to manage my own emotional response while being present for my team.","tags":["leadership","change-management","management"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-83","title":"Giving Feedback That Actually Changes Behavior","prompt":"You are a management coach specializing in feedback and performance conversations. Most managers give feedback in one of two broken ways: they are so gentle the message gets lost ('I just wanted to check in about...') or so blunt the person gets defensive and hears nothing. Effective feedback is specific, timely, behavior-based, and delivered with genuine care for the person's growth. I manage a team of 6 product designers. I have a mid-level designer who is technically strong but consistently misses deadlines, does not communicate proactively when she is stuck, and has created tension with two engineering partners. I have dropped hints and she has not changed. Walk me through: (1) the SBI feedback model (Situation-Behavior-Impact) and how to use it for this specific situation, (2) the opening for the conversation that creates psychological safety rather than defensiveness, (3) what to say when she gets defensive, (4) how to close with a specific, time-bound improvement agreement, and (5) how to document this conversation appropriately.","tags":["leadership","feedback","management"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-84","title":"Developing Your Leadership Point of View","prompt":"You are an executive development coach. The most effective senior leaders have a clear, articulated leadership point of view — a personal philosophy about how they lead, what they believe about people and performance, and what they stand for as a leader. This is not the same as listing your values. A leadership point of view is specific, earned from real experience, and often counterintuitive. I am a Director of Engineering who has been promoted quickly and wants to be more intentional about my leadership identity. Run me through a leadership philosophy development process: (1) five reflection questions that uncover my core leadership beliefs from specific experiences, (2) the structure of a compelling leadership statement (mine is not 'I value integrity and teamwork'), (3) how to articulate my philosophy in a team setting so it builds trust rather than sounding like a corporate poster, and (4) how to test and refine my philosophy in the next 6 months through deliberate situations.","tags":["leadership","executive-presence","self-awareness"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-85","title":"High-Stakes Presentation Prep System","prompt":"You are a communication coach and former TED talk advisor who has helped executives deliver transformative presentations to boards, investors, and thousands of people at conferences. Most professionals overprepare the content and underprepare the delivery — they build a beautiful deck and then stand behind it as a reader rather than a communicator. I have a board presentation in 3 weeks where I need to secure approval for a $4M investment in a new product line. This is the most important presentation of my career to date. Design a 3-week preparation system: (1) week 1 — structuring the argument and narrative arc so the board 'yes' comes before the Q&A, (2) week 2 — rehearsal methods that build real confidence rather than rote memorization, (3) week 3 — managing nerves, vocal presence, and the physical room, (4) how to handle hostile questions from the CFO who is skeptical of the ROI, and (5) what to do in the 30 minutes before you walk in the room.","tags":["leadership","presentations","executive-presence"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true},
    {"id":"car-86","title":"Building a High-Performance Team Culture","prompt":"You are an organizational psychologist and leadership coach. Building a genuinely high-performance team culture is one of the hardest and most rewarding things a leader can do — it requires clarity, consistency, psychological safety, and ruthless honesty about mediocre performance. I am 6 months into leading a 14-person sales team that inherited a culture of low accountability — people hit minimum targets but no one stretches, difficult conversations are avoided, and two senior reps seem to have more influence over the team mood than I do. Build me a culture reset plan: (1) the diagnostic I need to run to understand what is actually happening below the surface, (2) the three non-negotiable cultural norms I should establish first and how to enforce them without losing the team, (3) how to handle the two senior reps who are informal power centers, (4) how to create psychological safety and accountability at the same time (which most managers treat as opposites), and (5) the 6-month milestone for knowing the culture has genuinely shifted.","tags":["leadership","culture","management"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-87","title":"Delegation That Develops People","prompt":"You are a leadership development coach. Most managers either delegate too little (because it is faster to do it yourself) or delegate poorly (throwing tasks over the fence with no context or support). Effective delegation is one of the highest-leverage activities a manager can do — it develops your people, frees your time for strategic work, and creates a culture of ownership. I am a Head of Marketing who consistently works 60-hour weeks while my team of 7 feels underutilized. The root cause: I hold on to deliverables I know I could do better and faster myself. Help me redesign how I delegate: (1) the four levels of delegation and how to calibrate which level to use for each person and task, (2) the delegation conversation — how to hand something off in a way that transfers ownership rather than just the task, (3) how to resist the urge to take it back when someone does it differently than I would, (4) how to give the right amount of support without micromanaging, and (5) how to track delegated work without creating a surveillance culture.","tags":["leadership","delegation","management"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-88","title":"Difficult Conversation Preparation Guide","prompt":"You are an executive coach specializing in workplace communication. Avoiding difficult conversations is one of the most expensive things a leader can do — unaddressed performance issues compound, interpersonal tensions metastasize, and the team notices what you will not say. I need to have three difficult conversations this week: (1) telling a high-performer she is being paid below market and I cannot fix it immediately, (2) telling a long-tenured team member that his role is being eliminated in a restructure, and (3) telling my peer that his team's repeated lateness is damaging our shared client relationship and I need him to address it. For each conversation, write me: the exact opening line, the core message in plain language, how to handle the emotional reaction, and how to close with clarity about next steps. No corporate euphemisms. No weasel words.","tags":["leadership","communication","management"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-89","title":"Strategic Thinking Development for New Leaders","prompt":"You are a leadership coach who helps operational managers develop strategic thinking — the ability to step back from day-to-day execution and see the bigger picture. Most managers who struggle with promotion to director or VP level are told they are 'too tactical' but are never given concrete tools to shift their thinking. I am a senior manager who has been told by two different mentors that I need to develop my strategic thinking to be promotable. I am not sure what this actually means in practice. Break this down for me: (1) the concrete difference between tactical thinking and strategic thinking with examples from my likely day-to-day, (2) three daily habits that train the strategic thinking muscle, (3) how to redesign my weekly agenda to create time for strategic thinking rather than being consumed by operations, (4) how to contribute strategic ideas in a leadership meeting when I do not yet have all the information, and (5) the leading indicators that my strategic thinking is improving.","tags":["leadership","strategic-thinking","career-growth"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-90","title":"Managing a Team Remotely With Full Presence","prompt":"You are a remote leadership coach who has studied what separates remote teams that thrive from those that drift into disengagement and quiet quitting. Remote management is a fundamentally different discipline — you cannot rely on hallway conversations, ambient office culture, or reading body language in real time. I manage a fully remote team of 11 people across 4 time zones. My biggest challenges: I am not sure who is struggling until it is too late, team cohesion is low, and async communication creates misunderstandings and delays. Build me a remote leadership operating system: (1) the weekly rhythm of touchpoints that creates structure without over-meeting, (2) how to take the temperature of each team member's wellbeing without being intrusive, (3) the norms and working agreements that prevent async miscommunication, (4) how to build real psychological safety and belonging on a distributed team, and (5) how to make remote-first fair for team members across all time zones.","tags":["leadership","remote-work","management"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-91","title":"Imposter Syndrome at Senior Levels","prompt":"You are an executive coach and psychologist who specializes in high-achieving leaders. Imposter syndrome does not go away when you get promoted — for many high-achievers it intensifies as the stakes rise and the room gets smarter. I was just named Chief Product Officer at a Series C company. I am 38 years old, have never held a C-suite title, and am acutely aware that several people on the leadership team have more tenure and pattern recognition than I do. Help me work through this using evidence-based frameworks: (1) why imposter syndrome is more common at the top and what this says about cognitive sophistication rather than inadequacy, (2) the cognitive reappraisal technique for transforming imposter anxiety into performance-enhancing activation, (3) the specific daily practice that replaces the imposter narrative with one based on evidence and preparation, (4) how to use my 'outsider perspective' as a genuine strategic advantage in the new role, and (5) what I am allowed to not know yet — and how to be transparent about it with the team without losing authority.","tags":["leadership","mindset","executive-presence"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-92","title":"Sponsorship vs. Mentorship: Building Real Advocacy","prompt":"You are an executive coach specializing in career sponsorship and organizational power dynamics. Most professionals know they need a mentor but fewer understand the far more powerful role of a sponsor — someone who advocates for you when you are not in the room, uses their own political capital to open doors, and believes in you enough to bet their reputation on your potential. I am a Black woman at the Director level at a large financial services firm. I have excellent mentors but no sponsors. I know this is limiting my advancement. Help me understand and navigate sponsorship: (1) the precise difference between a mentor and a sponsor and why only sponsorship moves careers at the senior levels, (2) how sponsors are found vs. earned — and what earns a senior leader's advocacy, (3) how to strategically increase my visibility to potential sponsors without it feeling transactional or performative, (4) how to have a direct but appropriate conversation with someone I think could become a sponsor, and (5) what I owe a sponsor in return for their advocacy.","tags":["leadership","sponsorship","diversity"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

    // --- WORK-LIFE BALANCE & WELLBEING (93-100) ---
    {"id":"car-93","title":"Burnout Recovery and Prevention Plan","prompt":"You are a burnout recovery coach and organizational psychologist. Burnout is not a character flaw or a weakness — it is a predictable result of sustained high output without adequate recovery, autonomy, or meaning. I am a 35-year-old Director who has been operating at 110% for 18 months and just admitted to myself that I am burnt out. Symptoms: chronic fatigue, emotional flatness, cynicism about work I used to love, and difficulty concentrating. I cannot take 3 months off. Help me design a realistic burnout recovery plan that works within my current role: (1) the honest self-diagnosis — am I in early-stage or severe burnout and what is the difference, (2) the 3 root causes of burnout (workload, loss of control, lack of meaning) and how to diagnose which is primary for me, (3) the 30-day emergency recovery protocol I can start immediately without quitting, (4) the structural changes I need to make to my job over 90 days to prevent recurrence, and (5) when the right answer is to leave.","tags":["work-life-balance","wellbeing","burnout"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true},
    {"id":"car-94","title":"Boundaries at Work: Scripts for Every Situation","prompt":"You are a workplace boundaries coach. Setting boundaries at work is one of the most important and most avoided career skills — people fear being labeled difficult, uncommitted, or inflexible. Yet professionals without boundaries are the ones most likely to burn out, resent their colleagues, and eventually leave. I need practical boundary-setting scripts for the following five situations: (1) a manager who regularly sends messages after 9pm expecting immediate responses, (2) a colleague who consistently dumps their emergency work on me at 4:30pm on Fridays, (3) being asked to attend a meeting I am not needed at, (4) being assigned extra work without a conversation about what gets deprioritized, and (5) a client who calls my personal phone over weekends. For each situation, write a boundary-setting script that is firm, professional, and relationship-preserving. Then explain the principle behind each one.","tags":["work-life-balance","boundaries","communication"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-95","title":"Designing a Sustainable High-Performance Routine","prompt":"You are a high-performance coach who works with senior professionals and executives. The myth of sustainable high performance is that it requires longer hours. The research consistently shows that elite performance comes from deliberate effort combined with structured recovery — the same model used in elite athletics. I am a VP at a management consulting firm working 55–65 hours per week. My performance reviews are excellent but I am not thriving. I want to maintain my output while fundamentally redesigning how I work. Design me a sustainable high-performance operating system: (1) the 3-tier energy management model (physical, mental, emotional) and how to optimize each, (2) how to structure my workday around deep work vs. reactive work, (3) the non-negotiable recovery practices that have the highest ROI for cognitive performance, (4) how to protect personal time without creating problems with clients or leadership, and (5) the leading indicators that my routine is sustainable vs. slowly unsustainable.","tags":["work-life-balance","productivity","wellbeing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-96","title":"Managing Career Ambition and Personal Life","prompt":"You are a life and career integration coach who works with high-achieving professionals navigating the tension between career ambition and a meaningful personal life. Most career advice is written as if you exist only in a professional context. Real career decisions happen in the context of a partner, children, aging parents, health, identity, and values that extend beyond work. I am a 40-year-old General Manager at a retail company. I have a VP offer that would require significantly more travel and a longer commute. I have two children under 8, a partner who also works full-time, and I genuinely love my current lifestyle. Help me think through this with full life integration: (1) a values clarification exercise to surface what I actually care about at this stage of my life (not what I thought I cared about at 28), (2) a life-first decision matrix that weights career and personal factors with honest weights, (3) how to have an honest conversation with my partner about this decision as a joint life choice, and (4) the permission to choose the life I have without that being a career failure.","tags":["work-life-balance","decision-making","values"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-97","title":"Productivity System for Knowledge Workers","prompt":"You are a productivity coach specializing in knowledge workers — managers, executives, analysts, and creatives whose output is ideas, decisions, and communication rather than widgets. Most productivity systems are designed for factory work and collapse under the cognitive complexity of modern professional work. I am a senior analyst at a hedge fund. My work requires deep concentration for research, fast responses in a culture that values responsiveness, and strategic thinking — and I am failing at all three simultaneously because I am always context-switching. Build me a productivity architecture: (1) how to time-block my calendar to protect 3 hours of uninterrupted deep work daily in a culture that does not value it, (2) the inbox and communication management system that allows responsiveness without constant interruption, (3) a note-taking and knowledge management system that makes research compound over time, (4) how to end every workday with a shutdown ritual that creates real mental separation, and (5) the one habit change that will have the biggest impact in the first 30 days.","tags":["productivity","work-life-balance","focus"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-98","title":"Navigating Political Environments at Work","prompt":"You are an organizational politics expert and executive coach. Office politics is real, it is unavoidable, and pretending you are above it is a career-limiting strategy. The choice is not between 'playing politics' and 'being authentic' — it is between playing it naively and playing it ethically. I am a principal engineer at a large tech company who has just been passed over for a promotion I was clearly qualified for. When I dug into why, I learned that a peer who is less technically strong but far more politically savvy had campaigned for the promotion and I had simply done my work and waited. Help me understand what I missed and how to navigate this better going forward: (1) the anatomy of how promotions really happen at large companies vs. how we think they happen, (2) the specific political moves my peer likely made that I did not, (3) how to build organizational influence ethically — without compromising my integrity, (4) the relationship I need to build with my skip-level manager, and (5) how to regroup and position myself for the next promotion cycle.","tags":["career-planning","workplace","strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
    {"id":"car-99","title":"Professional Reinvention at Any Age","prompt":"You are a career reinvention coach who believes that the traditional model of one career for one lifetime is obsolete and that the ability to reinvent yourself professionally is the most important career skill of the 21st century. The average worker today will have 5–7 meaningful career transitions in their lifetime. I am 52 years old, have spent 25 years in print journalism, and am facing a structural decline in my industry that is not reversible. I am deeply skilled at research, long-form writing, synthesizing complex information, and interviewing — but my professional identity is tightly bound to a title (journalist) and an industry (media) that are contracting. Help me see myself differently: (1) reframe my skills in the language of the sectors that most value them — corporate communications, content strategy, research firms, legal, policy, (2) identify the 3 most realistic and financially viable landing zones given my background, (3) map the 90-day steps to credibility in the new space, and (4) help me write a new professional story that I actually believe. [Share any sectors or roles you have been curious about]","tags":["career-pivot","reinvention","personal-branding"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true},
    {"id":"car-100","title":"The Career Letter to Your Future Self","prompt":"You are a life design coach facilitating a powerful career reflection and visioning exercise. One of the most effective tools for career clarity and motivation is writing a letter from your future self — the version of you 5 years from now who has built exactly the career and life you wanted — back to the present. This exercise bypasses the analytical mind and accesses a deeper kind of knowing about what actually matters to you. Guide me through this exercise: (1) a 5-minute guided visualization to get into the mental state of the future self before writing, (2) a set of prompting questions to help me write the letter — covering professional achievements, personal growth, relationships built, and lessons learned, (3) a structure for the letter itself (200–300 words), and (4) instructions for how to use this letter as a practical planning document: what decisions does it clarify? What should I start, stop, or change? Run me through this exercise fully — do not just explain it. [Type 'Begin' and I will guide you through it step by step]","tags":["career-planning","self-reflection","life-design"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true}

];
  __exports.careerPrompts = careerPrompts;
})();